    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d131">

                <pb facs="0282" n="158" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg158"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">813.00/1105: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Spencer</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-09-30T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 30,
                                1921—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>37. Your despatch 185, August 16, 1921.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d131-f1"/></p>
                <p>Cable Department whether so-called interpretation of Monroe Doctrine, as
                    communicated to the Government of Salvador in February, 1920, by the Department
                    of State,<anchor n="40" xml:id="d131-f2"/> has been inserted in Central American
                    Constitution.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d131-f1" n="39" xml:id="d131-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d131-f2" n="40" xml:id="d131-f2-content">
                <p>See note of Feb. 26, 1920, to the Salvadoran Minister, <ref
                        target="frus1920v02#pg_226"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1920, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 226</ref>.</p>
            </note>
